## Where do these versions come from in Playwright (Node.js world)?

```json
"@playwright/test": "^1.57.0",
"@types/node": "^25.0.9",
"@serenity-js/cli": "^3.35.1",
"@serenity-js/core": "^3.35.1",
"@serenity-js/serenity-bdd": "^3.35.1",
"@serenity-js/playwright": "^3.35.1"
```

These **do NOT come from Playwright itself**.
They come from **npm (Node Package Manager)**.

👉 **npm registry = Maven Central (Java equivalent)**

---

## Java vs Playwright dependency world (simple mapping)

| Java                     | Playwright / Node.js                 |
| ------------------------ | ------------------------------------ |
| Maven Central Repository | **npm registry**                     |
| `pom.xml`                | **`package.json`**                   |
| `<dependency>`           | **`dependencies / devDependencies`** |
| `mvn install`            | **`npm install` / `npm i`**          |
| groupId + artifactId     | **package name**                     |
| version                  | **version**                          |

---

## Where exactly do we find these dependencies?

### 👉 Official source: **[https://www.npmjs.com](https://www.npmjs.com)**

This is the **single source of truth**.

---

## Example 1: Playwright

Search on npm:

```
@playwright/test
```

You’ll find:

* Latest version
* Install command
* Documentation
* Changelog

Install command shown on npm:

```bash
npm install -D @playwright/test
```

---

## Example 2: Serenity + Playwright

Search:

```
@serenity-js/playwright
```

You’ll see:

* Compatible Serenity versions
* Installation instructions
* Peer dependencies

Install command:

```bash
npm install -D @serenity-js/core @serenity-js/cli @serenity-js/serenity-bdd @serenity-js/playwright
```

📌 That’s how those versions entered your `package.json`.

